Put the title bar and the status bar away while a phone shows the keyboard in landscape.

With the soft keyboard up on a phone held in landscape the page is about 122px tall
(measured on an API 36 emulator with a 411dp wide screen). The title bar takes 35px, the
editor tab strip 33px and the status bar 28px, so the editor body had room for one line of
code, and the line being typed could be half under the keyboard.

On Android, Layout#layout now treats a window shorter than 200px as short. The title bar and
the status bar are then hidden through the same grid views the settings drive, which gives
the editor 79px (four lines) and puts the line being typed in view. They return once the
window is 240px or taller; the gap between the two numbers keeps a keyboard that is still
settling from toggling them. The tab strip stays: it names the file and shows its unsaved dot.

What the user chose is not overwritten. LayoutStateKeys.STATUSBAR_HIDDEN keeps the setting and
the status bar is drawn again only if the setting has it shown, and every site that asked
shouldShowCustomTitleBar for the title bar now asks Layout#shouldShowTitleBar, which adds the
short window to the answer, so a settings change while the keyboard is up cannot bring it back.

Status bar visibility also reports the temporary main-window hide, so editor dimensions and
notification sizing reclaim its height. Auxiliary title and status bars keep their own window's
visibility while the main window is short. The status bar command toggles the saved setting
instead of the temporary rendered visibility.

